WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
Speaker 1: You know, ever since we started hearing&nbsp;
about the labor shortage situation here in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:09.120
U.S., there's been so much blame to go around.&nbsp;
Some people like to blame lazy millennials. Other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:14.560
people like to blame the Joe Biden administration.&nbsp;
Other people like to blame poor people who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.800
just unwilling to go get a job. And you know,&nbsp;
there's been this idea that because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.880
boosted unemployment benefits, that meant that&nbsp;
many people were just one willing to get a job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.040
They wanted to just stay home and live off of&nbsp;
government resources. The same was said about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.120
the stimulus checks with the idea that, OK, well,&nbsp;
if you get a thousand dollars here and there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:34.640
you don't really need to work, which is kind of&nbsp;
silly, really, because obviously a thousand bucks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:40.400
or 100 bucks, $4800, that's not going to be enough&nbsp;
to cover your long term basic needs. And indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.680
the labor shortage does persist. The number of job&nbsp;
openings was at an all time high. As of October,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.280
at more than 11 million openings and companies&nbsp;
are struggling still to hire enough workers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.480
meet the demand. But the truth is that the labor&nbsp;
shortage is caused by a multitude of factors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.280
and chief among them have to do with baby&nbsp;
boomers retiring early and a reduction in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.760
worker visas that have been granted over the&nbsp;
course of the pandemic. Overall, I was able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:11.360
come up with eight reasons in total for the labor&nbsp;
shortage situation, so I wanted to run through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:15.280
them all with you here. So number one, and&nbsp;
these are in no particular order people are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.240
retiring. Early estimates suggest that the U.S.&nbsp;
has about two million more retirees right now than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.760
we otherwise would have expected if we had not&nbsp;
had the pandemic. And this makes sense because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:31.360
let's say you were 62 years old when the pandemic&nbsp;
started and you planned on retiring at age 65.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:36.160
Well, by now you're about sixty three and a half&nbsp;
going on 64. Do you really want to go back to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:42.080
work for another year or so, especially when you&nbsp;
already are semi-retired, as it is? Probably not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.400
especially given the fact that the stock market&nbsp;
has boomed over the past couple of years so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.280
people's retirement accounts are higher than they&nbsp;
expected them to be. Also, real estate values have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:56.400
gone up significantly, so people assuming they&nbsp;
own their house and assuming they have a 401k&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.280
or an IRA, they've seen a sizable growth in&nbsp;
their wealth over the past couple of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.400
making it so they may not have to keep working.&nbsp;
They might as well just retire early. Number two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.600
has to do with immigration. The U.S. government&nbsp;
has issued about 1.2 million fewer visas to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.720
adult immigrants, refugees and temporary foreign&nbsp;
workers. And I think this is intuitive given that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.440
restrictions on travel were in existence,&nbsp;
especially during the early phase of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.480
pandemic. Many of these restrictions still exist&nbsp;
today. There's a chance also that you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.880
things can turn around because now the Department&nbsp;
of Homeland Security has announced that the Biden&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.600
administration is going to be adding 20000 new&nbsp;
slots to a seasonal guest worker program for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.920
the winter season. But it makes sense that given&nbsp;
there's a pandemic raging throughout the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.120
people just don't want to be traveling&nbsp;
from country to country. Number three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.160
there's a skills mismatch of 48 percent of workers&nbsp;
said that they were frustrated with the surge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:53.600
and 46 percent said that they can only find&nbsp;
Low-paying roles just because a job is available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.520
That doesn't mean that people necessarily want&nbsp;
to take that job. It doesn't necessarily mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.320
that it's a good paying job. And we know that some&nbsp;
people were able to take advantage of their time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:08.800
off during the pandemic, and many people learn new&nbsp;
skills. They did online classes, et cetera. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.560
their market value has improved. But if they don't&nbsp;
find a job that matches their skill improvement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:20.560
maybe they're just going to decide to hold out.&nbsp;
No, for commuting people many times just don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.280
want to go back to commuting. Obviously, during&nbsp;
the beginning phase of the pandemic, many workers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:30.080
moved out of the areas that would be hiring by&nbsp;
now, like they moved from cities to rural areas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:34.080
and they just have decided they don't want to&nbsp;
commute anymore. That's been my experience. Like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.640
I like working from home. It wouldn't be a total&nbsp;
deal breaker for me if I had to start going back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:42.880
into an office every day, but I'd prefer&nbsp;
to just keep working from home. Obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.040
commuting can be very, very stressful, especially&nbsp;
if you have a long commute, like if you're driving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.520
an hour to and from work every day. I mean, that's&nbsp;
just crazy. If you don't have to do it anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.400
why would you want to? So commuting obviously&nbsp;
is a big factor. Number five, child care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.760
Most schools are open at this point, but there&nbsp;
can be cancelations based on COVID cases at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:06.320
drop of a hat. Maybe the school year gets halted&nbsp;
early. Obviously, we're seeing some closures now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:12.000
with Macron. So sometimes parents who may be&nbsp;
working part time while the kids are at school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.840
they can't do that anymore because they have&nbsp;
to be home to take care of the child. Or at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:21.040
least they have to keep that as an option in case&nbsp;
school does get shut down. Number six is protests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.440
over vaccine mandates. There are some people&nbsp;
who are just not going to get the vaccine no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.120
matter what. It doesn't matter if you offer&nbsp;
them a carrot or a stick. And so obviously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:33.040
if they refuse to get the vaccine, there are&nbsp;
so many jobs that they're no longer eligible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:37.280
for. They can't work for a government agency&nbsp;
or they can't be a government contractor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.880
They can't work for a company with more than&nbsp;
50 employees. They can't work in certain cities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.200
in the country. So obviously that lowers their&nbsp;
possibilities when it comes to the job market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:52.320
and that's also contributing in part to the labor&nbsp;
shortage. Number seven Fear of the virus cases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:57.040
are obviously surging out of control right&nbsp;
now. The al-Muqrin variant is super. Tejas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:02.320
and so there are people who are maybe older&nbsp;
or immunosuppressed, and they feel as though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:07.760
you know, I want to get a job, but I don't want to&nbsp;
put my life at risk by doing so. And number eight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:12.880
the great resignation. This is a term coined&nbsp;
by organizational psychologist Anthony Klotz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:18.080
who theorizes that people, especially&nbsp;
pre-pandemic, they would tie in their identity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.200
with their work. And now with people taking&nbsp;
some time off and really being confronted with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.200
their mortality because the amount of people&nbsp;
who have died over the past couple of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:32.160
it's caused a shift in their perspective and they&nbsp;
no longer wrap up their identity with what they do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:37.040
as a job. And I think that, you know, maybe the&nbsp;
great resignation talking point is a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:42.880
overblown because after all, people need to work&nbsp;
to survive. Most people do anyway. But you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:47.120
there could be some people who decide, I'm not&nbsp;
going to work 40 to 50 hours a week anymore. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:51.680
going to work part time. Or maybe I'll take a few&nbsp;
more years off and resume working later. Maybe I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:57.120
want to decide that I'm going to do something&nbsp;
else with my life. So it's definitely a mix of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.040
factors. Some of these things you're going to&nbsp;
be able to improve over time, other things you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:05.520
may not be able to like, for example, the Baby&nbsp;
Boomers retiring early. Are you really going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:09.600
be able to bring them back into the job market?&nbsp;
Probably not. They're probably retired and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.160
going to be entering the job force again, at least&nbsp;
not in mass. But then there's other factors that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.600
will improve as the state of coronavirus in this&nbsp;
country improves. As we finally see cases go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:24.320
down and we finally start to see deaths go down&nbsp;
whenever that may be, the economy will follow.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:28.320
We've been saying this for two years now that you&nbsp;
have to deal with the virus before you can deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:33.520
with the economy. And we'll hope that this all&nbsp;
sort of sorts itself out. But that's the sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.040
update now having to do with the labor shortage&nbsp;
and many of the reasons why it's happening.

